*Exit* | V-$CHIPVR ($CHIPCN)

A program may exit in one of two ways:

- it *falls off the end* of the code, which exits the program with exit code ''0'',
- or a *global-scope ''return''* halts it, the ''return''ed value in this case, being the exit code

*Exit Code*

A ''return'' is global-scope whenever it sits outside any function, including inside a top-level block. Inside a function, a ''return'' hands the ''return''ed value back to the caller. It does not exit the program.

A global-scope ''return'' ends the process with its value as the exit code. A file pulled in with ''load(filename)'' runs inside the same program, so a global-scope ''return'' in it ends the program.

The value must be an integer between 0 and 255, any other value will cause a runtime error at the ''return'' statement. A bare ''return'' without a value exits with ''0''.

*Actors*

A ''return'' inside an actor is a function-scope ''return'': it ends the actor with the ''return''ed value. It does not exit the program.

The same is true for a global-scope ''return'' in a file pulled in with ''load(filename)'' from inside an actor. The actor ends with that value and ''wait(handle)'' ''return''s it to the spawner.

Value limitations do not apply in the case of function-scope ''return''s, the ''return'' value can be anything.

